  "description": "Faithful replication of Study 3 with the original photos: four photomontages of the same tree-lined city road with a tram line, built by crossing two factors, a red pop-up bike lane or ordinary car lanes, with or without people using them (A: bike lane with cyclists, B: bike lane only, C: car lanes with cars, D: car lanes only). As in the paper, each participant first says how often they use a car as a driver, then reads a thought experiment (they have just moved to a new city and step out onto the street for the first time), sees one of the four photos drawn at random for at least 8 seconds, says what they would do on their first day in the new district, and rates how often the people living in this district use the car, the bicycle, public transport and walking for commuting, shopping and everyday trips, all on the paper's six-point frequency scale from 'almost never' to 'almost daily'. Rebuilt from Rollin & Bamberg (2021), It's All Up to My Fellow Citizens. Descriptive Norms as a Decisive Mediator in the Relationship Between Infrastructure and Mobility Behavior, Frontiers in Psychology 11: 610343, with the photos from its Figure 4 (photos: Michael Motyka) and the procedure and items from the Study 1-3 methods (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: photo_A to photo_D as in Figure 4; answers are in car_use, first_day (free text) and norms (item codes car_, bike_, pt_, walk_ followed by commute, shop or everyday; 1 = almost never to 6 = almost daily), the last two keyed by image id; the paper's group sizes and means are stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: average the three car items into a car-use descriptive norm and the nine bicycle, public transport and walking items into an eco-mobility descriptive norm, compare the four photos with one-way ANOVA and Tukey post hoc tests, correlate both norms with own car use, and code the first-day answers for mentions of cycling or driving. The paper (276 German online-panel participants in 2020, 65-71 per photo) found that both norms differed between the photos: the car-lane photos raised the car-use norm and the bike-lane photos raised the eco-mobility norm, the bike-lane photo with cyclists gave the highest eco-mobility norm, cycling was mentioned as a first-day activity only after the bike-lane photos, and own car use was not linked to the norms people read from the photos. Differences from the original study: (1) the paper ran in German with a commercial panel; here the survey is in English and anyone can take part; (2) the paper prints one example norm item ('The people living in my city district use the car for commuting'); the other eleven items are written by us from its four modes and three trip purposes, with 'this district' for the new district; (3) the paper's answer option 'less three times a week' is printed as 'less than three times a week'; (4) the paper does not say whether the photo stayed on screen during the questions; here it stays visible above them; (5) the paper does not print its demographic questions; only age and gender are asked; (6) the photos are the figure copies (about 360 x 360 px).",
